<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Eastern Zhou</h1><p class="st-meta">770 BCE – 256 BCE · capital Luoyang</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The age of the Zhou kings at Luoyang, whose real power faded while the Spring and Autumn and Warring States periods produced Confucius, Laozi and the Hundred Schools of Thought.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quanrong">Quanrong</a> sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haojing">Haojing</a> in 771 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-ping-of-zhou">King Ping</a> moved the court east to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyi</a> (Luoyang), under the protection of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-jin">State of Jin</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-zheng">State of Zheng</a>, beginning the Eastern Zhou.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The kings kept their ritual supremacy but lost real authority. In the Spring and Autumn period (770 to 476 BCE) hegemons such as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/state-of-qi">State of Qi</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-jin">State of Jin</a> led the states in the king's name; in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a> the great states fought wars of annihilation. Iron tools, coinage, cities and bureaucracies spread, and thinkers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-fei">Han Feizi</a> debated how to restore order.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The royal domain split into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-western-zhou">Duchy of Western Zhou</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-eastern-zhou">Duchy of Eastern Zhou</a>. In 256 BCE the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/state-of-qin">State of Qin</a> seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wangcheng">Wangcheng</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-nan-of-zhou">King Nan</a>, the last Zhou king, died. Its intellectual legacy shaped Chinese civilisation for the next two thousand years.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/eastern-zhou.jpg" alt="Eastern Zhou" width="320" height="408"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:China_2b.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>770 BCE – 256 BCE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Luoyang</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>King Ping of Zhou, King Nan of Zhou</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Zhou ancestral religion and worship of Heaven</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Zhou dynasty (Eastern)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-513&amp;focus=eastern-zhou" aria-label="Eastern Zhou on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7535 923 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Eastern Zhou lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7855 1179L7768 1193M7855 1179L7768 1187M7855 1179L7969 1105M7855 1179L7867 1006M7855 1179L7894 1205M7855 1179L7932 655"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7768 1193h0M7768 1187h0M7969 1105h0M7867 1006h0M7894 1205h0M7932 655h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7855 1179h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7855 1179h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Luoyang. <a href="/atlas?year=-513&amp;focus=eastern-zhou">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
